---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('sequenzy')!;
const b = appBySlug('klaviyo')!;
const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should an existing Klaviyo store evaluate Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "When a concrete lifecycle requirement or operating burden remains unresolved. Compare the proposed Sequenzy sequence against the installed Klaviyo flows, including historical segments, offers, exclusions and reporting. A new tool is not automatically a better strategy."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do cart and checkout flows avoid overlapping?",
    "answer": "Klaviyo documents excluding people who start checkout from an Added to Cart flow, alongside the purchase exclusion. Demonstrate equivalent ownership across the flows you intend to keep in either stack."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a checkout event prove abandonment?",
    "answer": "No. A shopper may complete the order after entering. Check the purchase exclusion before later messages, and investigate event synchronization when post-purchase reminders appear."
  },
  {
    "question": "What about Shopify draft orders?",
    "answer": "Klaviyo documents draft orders as a special source of Started Checkout activity and recommends a trigger filter for their placeholder item. Inspect actual event contents before applying that exclusion; do not hide legitimate storefront checkouts."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should a high-value basket get a larger code?",
    "answer": "Basket value alone is insufficient. Calculate product cost and discount loss, then account for fulfillment, fees and expected incrementality. A discount may leave very little product gross profit on a low-margin basket."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can migration performance be compared immediately?",
    "answer": "Imported history, changed eligible populations and overlapping attribution can distort the comparison. Reconcile entry events and unique orders first, and use a planned experiment if the question is incremental impact."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Decide whether to maintain an existing Klaviyo recovery system or rebuild its lifecycle requirements in Sequenzy. Start with flow overlap and the history behind each branch.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Evaluate Sequenzy', tool: a.name, reason: 'Connected Shopify sequences when they solve an identified lifecycle need.' }, { label: 'Evaluate the installed setup', tool: b.name, reason: 'Keep useful flows and historical logic visible in the switching decision.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Compare the system you operate, not two feature labels</h2>
        <p>A store with Klaviyo already in place may have browse reminders, cart messages, checkout recovery, welcome offers and customer-history branches. Replacing only the cart template leaves the surrounding logic unresolved. The useful question is whether Sequenzy can meet those specific requirements with less work or better control, and whether the benefit justifies rebuilding and checking them.</p>
        <p>Sequenzy's <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/features/shopify-integration">Shopify integration overview</a> describes customer and order sync, email sequences and purchase-based segmentation. Those are useful reasons to evaluate it for connected lifecycle work. They do not prove an automatic migration of Klaviyo flows or better recovery results.</p>
        <p>Klaviyo's <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/35510464880027">Shopify Added to Cart guide</a> distinguishes earlier cart recovery from the default checkout-triggered flow. Its <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115002779411">abandoned-cart guide</a> describes purchase exclusions; its <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/12278373016603">metric-flow troubleshooting guide</a> explains the Shopify draft-order exception. Sources checked November 24, 2026. These published instructions do not establish the configuration or performance of your account.</p>
        <h2>Draw the flow hierarchy before changing a sender</h2>
        <p>List each active flow with its entry event, intended audience, messages, incentives, exclusions and owner. Keep Shopify's own reminders and any separate SMS sender in the list. When a shopper moves from browsing to adding an item to checkout, decide which flow now owns the recovery job. A new stage should not simply add another reminder on top of every earlier stage.</p>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Existing Klaviyo setup</th><th>Sequenzy demonstration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Cart becomes checkout</td><td>Inspect the cart flow's checkout and purchase exclusions.</td><td>Show how the earlier sequence stops or becomes ineligible when checkout recovery takes ownership.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopper completes purchase</td><td>Inspect the purchase filter and synced order event, rather than relying on the flow name.</td><td>Trace the order through every active recovery branch and pending message.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Returning customer enters</td><td>Record the historical fields and offer eligibility behind the installed branch.</td><td>Show the imported history before the corresponding sequence decision.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operator changes a condition</td><td>Identify every flow that depends on the condition.</td><td>Demonstrate the work needed to update the same policy without conflicting messages.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Proposed audit: one shopper passes through several stages</h2>
        <p>Use a designated test profile and inspect the event timeline and recipient activity together. These are proposed acceptance cases, not experiments this site has run.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Add an item, then start checkout:</strong> confirm which entry events arrive and which flow remains eligible. Klaviyo's cart guide specifies both checkout and purchase exclusions for the earlier cart flow. Demonstrate the same intended hierarchy in a replacement rather than assuming sequence names enforce it.</li>
          <li><strong>Buy while a reminder waits:</strong> check the order event and the reason later messages skip. If the order is missing, investigate sync; if it exists but messages remain eligible, inspect the filter. Do not solve a filter problem by blindly extending every delay.</li>
          <li><strong>Use an existing customer's record:</strong> compare prior purchases, subscription state and welcome-offer history before the branch runs. A historical purchase import must not create a new welcome entitlement or enroll old carts as fresh abandonment.</li>
          <li><strong>Change the basket:</strong> inspect product images, variant details, prices and the checkout destination in the actual pending message. A valid trigger does not prove that a copied dynamic block still references the right event.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeat the event:</strong> inspect re-entry and duplicate handling. Keep the event and checkout identifiers needed to distinguish a retry from a new shopping session. Avoid merging two legitimate baskets merely because the email address matches.</li>
        </ol>
        <h2>Keep draft orders out of the wrong journey</h2>
        <p>A merchant-created draft-order path can resemble checkout activity without being the storefront recovery opportunity the flow was designed for. Klaviyo documents Shopify draft orders producing Started Checkout activity without a corresponding Placed Order event in that path, and identifies a placeholder item beginning “Amount to pay for order.” Its guide recommends a trigger exclusion for those items.</p>
        <p>Inspect a real draft-order event alongside a normal storefront checkout in your own account. Apply the exclusion to the intended path and confirm that legitimate baskets still qualify. For a Sequenzy evaluation, include a merchant-created draft order in the demonstration and record whether and how it appears. The Klaviyo documentation is not evidence that another integration emits the same event or placeholder.</p>
        <h2>A low-margin basket changes the incentive decision</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $797 basket at 32% product gross margin has $541.96 product cost and $255.04 full-price product gross profit. A 17% code removes $135.49 from revenue, leaving $661.51 revenue and $119.55 product gross profit with the same cost. This is before fulfillment, payment fees, messaging and returns. The discount consumes more than half the original product gross profit; it does not reduce product cost by 17%.</p>
        <p>For that kind of basket, test whether delivery information, fit guidance or payment assistance resolves the problem before proposing an incentive. Follow the existing signup promise and checkout rules when an offer is already available. A high order value alone is not a reason to increase the code or add urgency unsupported by inventory or an actual deadline.</p>
        <h2>Separate migration checks from revenue claims</h2>
        <p>First compare event coverage, branch selection, exclusions and content against the requirements. A flow can skip purchasers correctly and still show fewer attributed orders than a competing report. Reconcile unique order identifiers, refunds, coupon cost and attribution windows before interpreting the totals. Cart and checkout populations also differ, so a change in trigger can change apparent performance without improving the message.</p>
        <p>If the goal is causal lift, plan a controlled comparison using expected eligible volume and the normal buying cycle. Choose an evaluation window and meaningful outcome before reviewing results. No universal five-percent holdout or fixed number of days can establish adequate evidence for every store.</p>
        <h2>Make the switching cost explicit</h2>
        <p>Quote the same required contact population, email usage, automation features and support in the current plans. Add data migration, connector work, template rebuilding, subscription overlap and staff maintenance. If the design includes SMS, price the required supplier, usage and permission handling separately; do not infer equivalent channel capability from this email comparison.</p>
        <p>Before cutover, reconcile suppression and customer history, then choose whether old eligible messages drain or are cancelled. Activate one recovery owner at a time. Keep a rollback map showing which entries and queued messages must stop before the previous sender resumes. An arbitrary parallel-running period can create duplicate reminders instead of useful validation.</p>
        <p>Retain Klaviyo when the installed hierarchy works and no concrete replacement benefit has been demonstrated. Consider Sequenzy when connected lifecycle sequences meet the store's requirements and the demonstrated operating benefit exceeds the migration cost. Request the event-path demonstration before treating either vendor as the default winner.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy vs Klaviyo FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
